1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
El Dorado was not a city.

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:05,427
It was a man — naked,
covered in gold dust,

3
00:00:05,427 --> 00:00:09,400
standing on a raft in the
middle of a mountain lake.

4
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:12,860
The best-known account
was written in 1636 by

5
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:16,520
Juan Rodríguez Freyle, a
chronicler from Bogotá.

6
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:21,320
He had it from a friend: don Juan,
the Muisca lord of Guatavita,

7
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:24,000
whose uncle ruled when
the Spaniards arrived.

8
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:27,740
Don Juan was in the middle of
an heir's fast at that moment.

9
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,120
That was the custom, Freyle writes.

10
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,900
An heir spent six years in a cave.

11
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:36,560
No women, no meat, no salt, no chili.

12
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:40,620
He came out only at night, to
look at the moon and the stars.

13
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:42,860
Then came the lake.

14
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:47,400
On Lake Guatavita the people built
a great raft of reeds and set four

15
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:51,780
braziers on it, burning incense
until the smoke dimmed the daylight.

16
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:55,840
Crowds ringed the shore in
feathers and gold crowns.

17
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:59,160
They stripped the heir,
smeared him with sticky earth,

18
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:03,480
and dusted him with powdered gold
until he was covered from head to foot.

19
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:06,040
He stood on the raft
with a heap of gold and

20
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:09,500
emeralds at his feet and
four chiefs beside him.

21
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:14,060
Horns sounded and the shore roared until
they reached the middle of the lake.

22
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:18,760
Then a flag went up for silence, and
he threw the gold into the water.

23
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:20,040
The chiefs threw theirs.

24
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:24,020
When the flag came down,
the noise began again.

25
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:25,680
He was lord.

26
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:27,920
It was not treasure being hoarded.

27
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,300
It was a gift.

28
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:32,180
The Muisca got most of
their gold through trade,

29
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:36,300
and what they made of it they gave
to their gods, in lakes and rivers.

30
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:40,720
The Spaniards heard of it in Quito,
from a man of the Bogotá country.

31
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,760
"Let us go and find this
gilded Indian," said

32
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:46,480
the conquistador Sebastián de Belalcázar.

33
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:49,560
Three Spanish captains
reached the highlands.

34
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:51,820
They found gold and emeralds.

35
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:53,980
They did not find a golden city.

36
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,300
The name soon left the man.

37
00:01:56,420 --> 00:02:00,360
It became a place, then a city,
then a lake called Parime,

38
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:03,500
drawn on European maps
for two hundred years.

39
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:06,040
Walter Raleigh sailed after it twice;

40
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:10,280
the second time his son died, and
the king had Raleigh executed.

41
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:15,120
Freyle called it a name that "has
cost so many lives and fortunes."

42
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:17,780
The lake kept most of its secrets.

43
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:22,500
Antonio de Sepúlveda won a licence from
Philip the Second to drain it, cut a

44
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:27,440
channel, and said he took more than twelve
thousand pesos from the exposed shore.

45
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,880
Years later he tried to drain
it deeper and could not.

46
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:34,860
He died poor, and Freyle, who
knew him, helped bury him.

47
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:37,400
A clue turned up in a cave.

48
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:43,060
In 1969 a farmer at Pasca found gold
and pottery and told his priest.

49
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:48,320
Among it was a gold raft twenty
centimeters long: a seated chief,

50
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,520
ten smaller figures around him,
some masked, all cast in one pour.

51
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:59,400
Charcoal from its mold dates it to
about 1300 to 1400, before Columbus.

52
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:05,780
Whether it shows Guatavita, scholars argue;
it was found at Pasca, not by that lake.

53
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:10,220
And whether the ceremony happened as
Freyle tells it, nobody can prove.

54
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:12,960
He wrote it a century after the conquest,

55
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:15,980
and the Spaniards who
came hunting never saw it.

56
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:19,160
Freyle went hunting himself,
and he confesses it.

57
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:24,720
He wanted one of two golden caimans said
to lie in another sacred lake, Teusacá.

58
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:29,460
He flattered a priest who had once kept
that shrine, and the priest led him there.

59
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:33,900
When the water came into view, the
priest fell face down on the ground.

60
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:37,960
Freyle could not raise him, and
never got another word out of him.

61
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:41,700
"I left him there," he wrote,
"and went back with nothing."

62
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,140
He does not say why the man fell.

63
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:48,200
The Spaniards were looking for a
place where gold lay for the taking.

64
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:51,880
What the Muisca had was a
lake where it was given away.

65
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:56,480
The gold went into the lake, and
the legend went around the world.

66
00:03:56,780 --> 00:04:00,100
If you like legends with a
real story hidden inside,

67
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:03,000
leave a like and subscribe
to Animated Tales.

68
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:04,271
Until next time.
